"Internal Error ()\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2494\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2494 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFormal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol  (https://ch-sprenger.github.io/assets/pdf/csf2024-sumcheck-verification.pdf)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ch-sprenger.github.io/assets/pdf/csf2024-sumcheck-verification.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1723\nL0@P0: Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol\nL1@P0: Azucena Garv´ıa Bosshard\nL2@P0: Independent Researcher†\nL3@P0: azucena.garvia-bosshard@alumni.ethz.ch\nL4@P0: Jonathan Bootle\nL5@P0: IBM Research Europe\nL6@P0: jbt@zurich.ibm.com\nL7@P0: Christoph Sprenger\nL8@P0: ETH Zurich\nL9@P0: sprenger@inf.ethz.ch\nL10@P0: Abstract—The sumcheck protocol, introduced in 1992, is an\nL11@P0: interactive proof which is a key component of many probabilistic\nL12@P0: proof systems in computational complexity theory and cryptogra\u0002phy, some of which have been deployed. However, none of these\nL13@P0: proof systems based on the sumcheck protocol enjoy a formally\u0002verified security analysis. In this paper, we make progress in\nL14@P0: this direction by providing a formally verified security analysis\nL15@P0: of the sumcheck protocol using the interactive theorem prover\nL16@P0: Isabelle/HOL. We follow a general and modular approach.\nL17@P0: First, we give a general formalization of public-coin interactive\nL18@P0: proofs. We then define a generalized sumcheck protocol for which\nL19@P0: we axiomatize the underlying mathematical structure and we\nL20@P0: establish its soundness and completeness. Finally, we prove that\nL21@P0: these axioms hold for multivariate polynomials, the original\nL22@P0: setting of the sumcheck protocol. Our modular analysis facilitates\nL23@P0: formal verification of sumcheck instances based on different\nL24@P0: mathematical structures with little effort, by simply proving that\nL25@P0: these structures satisfy the axioms. Moreover, the analysis supports\nL26@P0: the development and formal verification of future cryptographic\nL27@P0: protocols using the sumcheck protocol as a building block.\nL28@P0: Index Terms—sumcheck protocol, formal verification, interac\u0002tive proofs, Isabelle\nL29@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL30@P0: Probabilistic proof systems are protocols in which a powerful\nL31@P0: but untrusted prover can convince a verifier that a given state\u0002ment is true. They differ from traditional mathematical proofs\nL32@P0: by incorporating interaction, randomness and cryptographic\nL33@P0: assumptions. As a result, they offer benefits such as fast\nL34@P0: verification and zero-knowledge properties.\nL35@P0: Early research into interactive proofs [1], [2] produced\nL36@P0: complexity theoretic results such as IP = PSPACE. Today,\nL37@P0: probabilistic proof systems enjoy a high degree of adoption\nL38@P0: and are increasingly deployed in applications such as verifiable\nL39@P0: computation, electronic voting, and cryptocurrencies.\nL40@P0: Errors in analysis. Unfortunately, the designs and deploy\u0002ments of probabilistic proof systems often contain errors:\nL41@P0: • The analysis of the proof system [3] for integer commitments\nL42@P0: contained errors, later addressed in [4];\nL43@P0: • The public setup information of the proof system [5]\nL44@P0: contained extra information which made the proof system\nL45@P0: insecure. This affected the ZCash cryptocurrency [6];\nL46@P0: • A significant gap in the security proof of [7] was found and\nL47@P0: fixed using three different methods in [8], [9], [10].\nL48@P0: • The proof system described in [11] states a cheating prob\u0002ability lower than its actual value. A proper analysis was\nL49@P0: given in [12], [13].\nL50@P0: † Work done while at ETH Zurich.\nL51@P0: • The description of the Fiat-Shamir transformation in [14] ne\u0002glects to hash certain values, which leads to the vulnerability\nL52@P0: described in [15]. This affected multiple implementations of\nL53@P0: proof systems [16], [17], [18], [19], [20].\nL54@P0: Other researchers have raised concerns about errors in\nL55@P0: cryptographic analyses in general [21], [22]. As probabilistic\nL56@P0: proofs have become increasingly popular, their security analyses\nL57@P0: demand greater care and scrutiny.\nL58@P0: Formal verification of probabilistic proof systems. In order\nL59@P0: to remedy these issues, researchers are starting to turn to\nL60@P0: machine-checkable security analyses using formal verification\nL61@P0: techniques. Existing formal verification work falls into two\nL62@P0: categories: formal verification of non-interactive proof systems,\nL63@P0: such as that of [23] by [24], [25], and constant-round interactive\nL64@P0: proof systems, including Sigma protocols [26], protocols using\nL65@P0: the “MPC-in-the-head” paradigm of [27] in [28], [29], and\nL66@P0: particular signature schemes [30], [31].\nL67@P0: However, despite this growing body of work, interactive\nL68@P0: proof systems with a non-constant number of rounds have not\nL69@P0: yet been addressed. This category contains a large number of\nL70@P0: protocols, including proof systems based on the GKR protocol\nL71@P0: [32] for uniform circuits, proof systems for NP [33], “split-and\u0002fold” proof systems such as [34], [14], as well as protocols with\nL72@P0: optimal prover complexity [35], [36], [37], [38] and interesting\nL73@P0: streaming properties [39], [40], [8], [41]. Note that while e.g.\nL74@P0: [26] does consider sequential composition of Sigma protocols,\nL75@P0: leading to a multi-round protocol, many of the protocols cited\nL76@P0: above have a significantly more complicated recursive structure.\nL77@P0: In fact, the multi-round protocols cited above are all based on\nL78@P0: a single protocol: the sumcheck protocol. Each protocol makes\nL79@P0: direct use of the sumcheck protocol, or its algebraic [42] or\nL80@P0: combinatorial [43] generalizations, as a subroutine. Moreover,\nL81@P0: the streaming properties of [40], [8], [41] are directly linked\nL82@P0: to those of the sumcheck protocol.\nL83@P0: The sumcheck protocol. As the name suggests, the goal of\nL84@P0: the sumcheck protocol is to check that the evaluation of a sum\nL85@P0: corresponds to a certain value. More specifically, the verifier\nL86@P0: V’s objective is to use the prover P to confirm that\nL87@P0: v = ∑\nL88@P0: (x1,...,xn)∈Hn\nL89@P0: p(x1,..., xn) , (1)\nL90@P0: where H is a subset of some field F, p is a polynomial of arity\nL91@P0: n over F, and v ∈ F. The protocol has n rounds of interaction\nL92@P0: between the prover and the verifier, and a recursive structure\nL93@P0-1: whereby statements about polynomials with arity n are reducedto statements about polynomials with smaller arity, repeatedly,\nL94@P1: until arity 0 is reached.\nL95@P1: As well as being used in many practical probabilistic proof\nL96@P1: systems, the sumcheck protocol and its generalizations are also\nL97@P1: a core component of many complexity-theoretic results, like\nL98@P1: coNP ⊆ IP, PSPACE = IP [44], [45], and results on PPAD\u0002hardness [46], [47], [48]. It is also one of few interactive proof\nL99@P1: systems that can be made non-interactive via the Fiat-Shamir\nL100@P1: transformation without relying on heuristics like the random\nL101@P1: oracle model [49].\nL102@P1: Given the versatility of the sumcheck protocol, it is clear\nL103@P1: that its formal analysis is highly desirable and constitutes the\nL104@P1: first step towards analysing a great many probabilistic proofs\nL105@P1: with unique and interesting properties.\nL106@P1: A. Our Work\nL107@P1: We provide a formally verified security analysis of the\nL108@P1: sumcheck protocol [50]. We start with generic definitions of\nL109@P1: (recursive, multi-round) public-coin interactive proofs and the\nL110@P1: associated soundness and completeness properties, which we\nL111@P1: refer to as security properties. Our definition is parameterized\nL112@P1: by prover and verifier functions and reduces the size of the\nL113@P1: problem instance in each recursive call. We then formalize a\nL114@P1: generalized sumcheck protocol that abstracts from the concrete\nL115@P1: mathematical structure of polynomials and we provide machine\u0002checkable mathematical proofs of its security properties in\nL116@P1: Isabelle/HOL. Our proofs thus cover an entire protocol family\nL117@P1: of which the concrete sumcheck protocol is a special case. We\nL118@P1: also show that the protocol and its properties are an instance\nL119@P1: of our generic definitions for public-coin interactive proofs.\nL120@P1: More precisely, our analysis proceeds in two steps.\nL121@P1: In the first step, we axiomatize the key properties of\nL122@P1: polynomials required by the protocol, formalize the generalized\nL123@P1: sumcheck protocol based on these axioms, and prove its\nL124@P1: security. We have made an effort to make the the axioms as\nL125@P1: weak as possible to ensure their wide applicability. For example,\nL126@P1: we make only minimal assumptions about the polynomials’\nL127@P1: algebraic structure, namely, that they form a commutative\nL128@P1: monoid over a finite support set. For the inductive proofs to\nL129@P1: succeed, the soundness and completeness theorems required\nL130@P1: several generalizations of the statements usually found in less\nL131@P1: formal accounts. For example, in the generalized soundness\nL132@P1: statement, the assumptions about the polynomial’s arity and\nL133@P1: degree must be generalized to ensure that the induction\nL134@P1: hypothesis is applicable.\nL135@P1: In a second step, we show that concrete multivariate\nL136@P1: polynomials satisfy our axioms. We do this based on an\nL137@P1: existing (concrete) formalization of multivariate polynomials in\nL138@P1: Isabelle [51], which we extend with additional definitions and\nL139@P1: results, e.g., about polynomial instantiation and the number of\nL140@P1: a polynomial’s roots. This establishes the axioms’ consistency\nL141@P1: and specializes the security proofs to the concrete protocol\nL142@P1: instance.\nL143@P1: B. Our Contributions\nL144@P1: Our main contributions are as follows.\nL145@P1: • We define and formalize a generalized version of the sum\u0002check protocol and prove its soundness and completeness. We\nL146@P1: then instantiate this protocol with multivariate polynomials.\nL147@P1: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first machine\u0002checkable security analysis for probabilistic proof systems\nL148@P1: with an input-size-dependent number of rounds.\nL149@P1: • Our general and modular analysis has several benefits.\nL150@P1: First, it simplifies proofs by decoupling the reasoning about\nL151@P1: the protocol from reasoning about concrete polynomial\nL152@P1: representations. Second, our general results can be used\nL153@P1: to obtain security proofs of sumcheck protocol variants [52],\nL154@P1: [42] relying on different but similar mathematical structures,\nL155@P1: such as, for example, rings or modules instead of finite\nL156@P1: fields. This can be achieved with minimal effort by proving\nL157@P1: that these structures satisfy the required axioms, with some\nL158@P1: minor changes. Finally, our work paves the way for the future\nL159@P1: analysis of the many aforementioned protocols which use\nL160@P1: the sumcheck protocol as a subroutine.\nL161@P1: C. Related Work\nL162@P1: Bailey and Miller [24], [25] formalized the security anal\u0002yses of various non-interactive proof systems in the Lean 3\nL163@P1: interactive theorem prover. They analyze [53], [54], [55], [56],\nL164@P1: [57], and the construction of [23] which has extremely low\nL165@P1: communication complexity and is widely deployed. All of\nL166@P1: these proof systems are built from cryptographic pairings and\nL167@P1: have very similar properties. Their security analysis takes place\nL168@P1: in a strong idealized model called the “algebraic group model”,\nL169@P1: which simplifies both pen-and-paper and machine security\nL170@P1: analysis. The analysis focuses on verifying equality between\nL171@P1: the coefficients of various multivariate polynomial expressions.\nL172@P1: Work on constant-round proof systems includes analyses\nL173@P1: of Sigma protocols and their composition properties in [26]\nL174@P1: and protocols using the “MPC-in-the-head” paradigm of\nL175@P1: [27] in [28], [29]. Many signature schemes are also based\nL176@P1: on constant-round proof systems, and machine-formalized\nL177@P1: signature schemes and implementations of this type include\nL178@P1: Schnorr [30] and Dilithium [31] signatures. All of these\nL179@P1: formalizations use EasyCrypt. The analysis of Sigma protocols\nL180@P1: is made more challenging through the use of computational\nL181@P1: assumptions and security definitions involving computationally\nL182@P1: bounded adversaries, and rewinding algorithms, which we do\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nstrategy.rs - source (https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/src/p3_sumcheck/strategy.rs.html)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/src/p3_sumcheck/strategy.rs.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2604\nL0: 【0†iframe】【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: ## Files\nL3: #\nL4: \nL5: p3_sumcheck/\nL6: strategy.rs\nL7: \nL8:     `【2†1】//! Sumcheck helpers: variable ordering, round coefficients, and the prover state.\nL9:     【3†2】//!\nL10:     【4†3】//! # Layout\nL11:     【5†4】//!\nL12:     【6†5】//! - `sumcheck_coefficients_{prefix,suffix}`: the two round-coefficient routines.\nL13:     【7†6】//! - `VariableOrder`: tag enum carrying inherent methods that dispatch to either routine.\nL14:     【8†7】//! - `SumcheckProver`: drives rounds over a paired product polynomial.\nL15:     【9†8】//! - `ReprSumcheckProver`: drives the same rounds with the tables in an isomorphic field.\nL16:     【10†9】\nL17:     【11†10】mod repr;\nL18:     【12†11】\nL19:     【13†12】use alloc::vec::Vec;\nL20:     【14†13】\nL21:     【15†14】use p3_challenger::fs::TranscriptField;\nL22:     【16†15】use p3_challenger::{FieldChallenger, GrindingChallenger};\nL23:     【17†16】use p3_field::{Algebra, ExtensionField, Field, PrimeCharacteristicRing, dot_product};\nL24:     【18†17】use p3_maybe_rayon::PARALLEL_ENABLED;\nL25:     【19†18】use p3_maybe_rayon::prelude::*;\nL26:     【20†19】use p3_multilinear_util::point::Point;\nL27:     【21†20】use p3_multilinear_util::poly::{Poly, PolyMaybePackedView};\nL28:     【22†21】pub use repr::{FromTable, IntoTranscriptField, ReprSumcheckProver};\nL29:     【23†22】\nL30:     【24†23】use crate::constraints::{Constraint, Statements};\nL31:     【25†24】use crate::product_polynomial::ProductPolynomial;\nL32:     【26†25】use crate::transcript::{ProverTranscript, SumcheckShape};\nL33:     【27†26】use crate::{SumcheckData, extrapolate_01inf};\nL34:     【28†27】\nL35:     【29†28】/// Input size at which the round-coefficient routines switch from serial to parallel execution.\nL36:     【30†29】///\nL37:     【31†30】/// # Why this value\nL38:     【32†31】///\nL39:     【33†32】/// - Below `2^14` paired elements, the rayon splitting and join overhead outweighs the parallel work.\nL40:     【34†33】/// - Above it, the fold-reduce amortises the splitting cost.\nL41:     【35†34】const PAR_THRESHOLD: usize = 1 << 14;\nL42:     【36†35】\nL43:     【37†36】/// Table length from which a threaded suffix binding writes over its own tables.\nL44:     【38†37】///\nL45:     【39†38】/// # Why this value\nL46:     【40†39】///\nL47:     【41†40】/// - In place, the blocks of a binding run in levels, one parallel dispatch per level.\nL48:     【42†41】/// - Below `2^23` entries those dispatches cost more than a fresh half-length table.\nL49:     【43†42】/// - Above it, the fresh table's page faults and release dominate instead.\nL50:     【44†43】///\nL51:     【45†44】/// A table bound in place keeps its buffer, until a round below this length replaces it.\nL52:     【46†45】///\nL53:     【47†46】/// Without the `parallel` feature there is no dispatch to amortise, so every length\nL54:     【48†47】/// binds in place regardless of this threshold.\nL55:     【49†48】const SUFFIX_IN_PLACE_THRESHOLD: usize = 1 << 23;\nL56:     【50†49】\nL57:     【51†50】/// Tile size for the chunked round-coefficient kernel.\nL58:     【52†51】///\nL59:     【53†52】/// Monty-31 spells out a single-reduction dot product per tile size.\nL60:     【54†53】/// The set of sizes that get one depends on the target:\nL61:     【55†54】///\nL62:     【56†55】/// ```text\nL63:     【57†56】///     scalar          every length\nL64:     【58†57】///     AVX2, AVX-512   2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\nL65:     【59†58】///     NEON            2, 3, 4, 5, 8\nL66:     【60†59】/// ```\nL67:     【61†60】///\nL68:     【62†61】/// So `8` is a single-reduction tile on every target.\nL69:     【63†62】/// Binary fields fold the modulus once at any tile size, so the choice is free there.\nL70:     【64†63】///\nL71:     【65†64】/// - Larger overruns the integer-multiply pipeline depth;\nL72:     【66†65】/// - Smaller dilutes the delayed-reduction win.\nL73:     【67†66】const K: usize = 8;\nL74:     【68†67】\nL75:     【69†68】/// Per-tile MAC: extends a `(constant, leading)` accumulator pair.\nL76:     【70†69】///\nL77:     【71†70】/// # Algorithm\nL78:     【72†71】///\nL79:     【73†72】/// Folding the active variable in `h(X) = sum_b f(X, b) * w(X, b)` gives:\nL80:     【74†73】///\nL81:     【75†74】/// ```text\nL82:     【76†75】///     constant += sum_i  w_lo[i] * e_lo[i]\nL83:     【77†76】///     leading  += sum_i  (w_hi[i] - w_lo[i]) * (e_hi[i] - e_lo[i])\nL84:     【78†77】/// ```\nL85:     【79†78】///\nL86:     【80†79】/// where `lo`, `hi` are the two faces of the active variable.\nL87:     【81†80】///\nL88:     【82†81】/// Each sum is one dot product over `K` pairs, reduced as late as the target permits.\nL89:     【83†82】/// A binary field folds the modulus once for the whole sum.\nL90:     【84†83】/// A Monty-31 packing pays one Montgomery reduction at this tile size on every target.\nL91:     【85†84】#[inline(always)]\nL92:     【86†85】fn chunk_round_step<B, A>(e_lo: &[B; K], e_hi: &[B; K], w_lo: &[A; K], w_hi: &[A; K]) -> (A, A)\nL93:     【87†86】where\nL94:     【88†87】    B: PrimeCharacteristicRing + Copy,\nL95:     【89†88】    A: Algebra<B> + Copy,\nL96:     【90†89】{\nL97:     【91†90】    // Constant term: one delayed-reduction dot product over the b_0 = 0 face.\nL98:     【92†91】    let acc0 = A::mixed_dot_product::<K>(w_lo, e_lo);\nL99:     【93†92】\nL100:     【94†93】    // Materialise the differences (b_0 = 1 minus b_0 = 0) tile-locally so\nL101:     【95†94】    // they can feed the same primitive. `K` base subs, no reductions.\nL102:     【96†95】    let diffs_e: [B; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| e_hi[i] - e_lo[i]);\nL103:     【97†96】    let diffs_w: [A; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| w_hi[i] - w_lo[i]);\nL104:     【98†97】\nL105:     【99†98】    // Leading coefficient: dot product of the differences.\nL106:     【100†99】    let acc_inf = A::mixed_dot_product::<K>(&diffs_w, &diffs_e);\nL107:     【101†100】\nL108:     【102†101】    (acc0, acc_inf)\nL109:     【103†102】}\nL110:     【104†103】\nL111:     【105†104】/// Per-pair MAC for the streaming tail (at most `K - 1` leftover pairs).\nL112:     【106†105】#[inline(always)]\nL113:     【107†106】fn round_step<B, A>((acc0, acc_inf): (A, A), e0: B, e1: B, w0: A, w1: A) -> (A, A)\nL114:     【108†107】where\nL115:     【109†108】    B: PrimeCharacteristicRing + Copy,\nL116:     【110†109】    A: Algebra<B> + Copy,\nL117:     【111†110】{\nL118:     【112†111】    (acc0 + w0 * e0, acc_inf + (w1 - w0) * (e1 - e0))\nL119:     【113†112】}\nL120:     【114†113】\nL121:     【115†114】/// Splits a `2K`-wide chunk into the two faces of the suffix round variable.\nL122:     【116†115】///\nL123:     【117†116】/// The suffix variable is the low index bit.\nL124:     【118†117】///\nL125:     【119†118】/// So the two faces of a point are adjacent entries:\nL126:     【120†119】///\nL127:     【121†120】/// ```text\nL128:     【122†121】///     chunk : [ t0, t1, t2, t3, ... ]\nL129:     【123†122】///     lo    : [ t0, t2, ... ]           the variable at 0\nL130:     【124†123】///     hi    : [ t1, t3, ... ]           the variable at 1\nL131:     【125†124】/// ```\nL132:     【126†125】#[inline(always)]\nL133:     【127†126】fn gather_pairs<T: Copy>(chunk: &[T]) -> ([T; K], [T; K]) {\nL134:     【128†127】    let lo: [T; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| chunk[2 * i]);\nL135:     【129†128】    let hi: [T; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| chunk[2 * i + 1]);\nL136:     【130†129】    (lo, hi)\nL137:     【131†130】}\nL138:     【132†131】\nL139:     【133†132】/// Component-wise sum of two `(constant, leading)` accumulator pairs.\nL140:     【134†133】#[inline(always)]\nL141:     【135†134】fn round_reduce<A: Copy + PrimeCharacteristicRing>(a: (A, A), b: (A, A)) -> (A, A) {\nL142:     【136†135】    (a.0 + b.0, a.1 + b.1)\nL143:     【137†136】}\nL144:     【138†137】\nL145:     【139†138】/// Projective per-tile MAC (eprint 2026/762, Fig. 3).\nL146:     【140†139】///\nL147:     【141†140】/// Like [`chunk_round_step`], but the tables are interpreted as monomial\nL148:     【142†141】/// coefficients, so the round message is `[s(1), s(inf)]` and the verifier\nL149:     【143†142】/// derives `s(0) := C - s(inf)` from the projective round identity. The\nL150:     【144†143】/// `X = 1` evaluation of a coefficient pair is `lo + hi`, so the differences\nL151:     【145†144】/// of the evaluation basis become sums:\nL152:     【146†145】///\nL153:     【147†146】/// ```text\nL154:     【148†147】///     at_one  += sum_i  (w_lo[i] + w_hi[i]) * (e_lo[i] + e_hi[i])\nL155:     【149†148】///     leading += sum_i  w_hi[i] * e_hi[i]\nL156:     【150†149】/// ```\nL157:     【151†150】#[inline(always)]\nL158:     【152†151】fn chunk_round_step_projective<B, A>(\nL159:     【153†152】    e_lo: &[B; K],\nL160:     【154†153】    e_hi: &[B; K],\nL161:     【155†154】    w_lo: &[A; K],\nL162:     【156†155】    w_hi: &[A; K],\nL163:     【157†156】) -> (A, A)\nL164:     【158†157】where\nL165:     【159†158】    B: PrimeCharacteristicRing + Copy,\nL166:     【160†159】    A: Algebra<B> + Copy,\nL167:     【161†160】{\nL168:     【162†161】    // Materialise the X = 1 evaluations (lo + hi) tile-locally so they can\nL169:     【163†162】    // feed the same delayed-reduction primitive. `K` base adds, no reductions.\nL170:     【164†163】    let ones_e: [B; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| e_lo[i] + e_hi[i]);\nL171:     【165†164】    let ones_w: [A; K] = core::array::from_fn(|i| w_lo[i] + w_hi[i]);\nL172:     【166†165】\nL173:     【167†166】    let acc1 = A::mixed_dot_product::<K>(&ones_w, &ones_e);\nL174:     【168†167】\nL175:     【169†168】    // Leading coefficient: dot product of the high (coefficient) faces.\nL176:     【170†169】    let acc_inf = A::mixed_dot_product::<K>(w_hi, e_hi);\nL177:     【171†170】\nL178:     【172†171】    (acc1, acc_inf)\nL179:     【173†172】}\nL180:     【174†173】\nL181:     【175†174】/// Projective per-pair MAC for the streaming tail (no subtraction).\nL182:     【176†175】#[inline(always)]\nL183:     【177†176】fn round_step_projective<B, A>((acc1, acc_inf): (A, A), e0: B, e1: B, w0: A, w1: A) -> (A, A)\nL184:     【178†177】where\nL185:     【179†178】    B: PrimeCharacteristicRing + Copy,\nL186:     【180†179】    A: Algebra<B> + Copy,\nL187:     【181†180】{\nL188:     【182†181】    (acc1 + (w0 + w1) * (e0 + e1), acc_inf + w1 * e1)\nL189:     【183†182】}\nL190:     【184†183】\nL191:     【185†184】/// The two prover-sent values of a quadratic sumcheck round.\nL192:     【186†185】///\nL193:     【187†186】/// The round polynomial has three unknowns; the verifier recovers the third\nL194:     【188†187】/// from the running claim `C`, so only two values cross the transcript. Which\nL195:     【189†188】/// finite point is sent differs by basis:\nL196:     【190†189】///\nL197:     【191†190】/// | basis      | `c_a`  | `c_inf`  | derived             |\nL198:     【192†191】/// |------------|--------|----------|---------------------|\nL199:     【193†192】/// | evaluation | `h(0)` | `h(inf)` | `h(1) = C - h(0)`   |\nL200:     【194†193】/// | projective | `s(1)` | `s(inf)` | `s(0) = C - s(inf)` |\nL201:     【195†194】///\nL202:     【196†195】/// The struct itself is basis-agnostic: `c_a` has the same name and type in\nL203:     【197†196】/// both rows, so it carries no evidence of which kernel produced it. What\nL204:     【198†197】/// keeps the rows from being crossed is that a message is only ever produced\nL205:     【199†198】/// by [`Basis::sumcheck_coefficients`] and only ever consumed by\nL206:     【200†199】/// [`Basis::reduce_claim`], each under the same tag.\nL207:     【201†200】#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nL208:     【202†201】pub struct RoundMessage<A> {\nL209:     【203†202】    /// The finite-point value: `h(0)` (evaluation basis) or `s(1)` (projective).\nL210:     【204†203】    pub c_a: A,\nL211:     【205†204】    /// The leading coefficient of the round polynomial: `h(inf)` / `s(inf)`.\nL212:     【206†205】    pub c_inf: A,\nL213:     【207†206】}\nL214:     【208†207】\nL215:     【209†208】/// Shared prefix round-coefficient scaffold, parameterised over the basis steps.\nL216:     【210†209】///\nL217:     【211†210】/// The tiling, `PAR_THRESHOLD` par-vs-serial split, and `K`-tail fold are\nL218:     【212†211】/// identical across bases; only the per-tile and per-pair MAC differ. The\nL219:     【213†212】/// evaluation and projective kernels supply their `chunk_step` / `pair_step`\nL220:     【214†213】/// pair so this delicate delayed-reduction loop exists exactly once.\nL221:     【215†214】#[inline]\nL222:     【216†215】fn sumcheck_coefficients_prefix_with<B, A, Chunk, Pair>(\nL223:     【217†216】    evals: &[B],\nL224:     【218†217】    weights: &[A],\nL225:     【219†218】    chunk_step: Chunk,\nL226:     【220†219】    pair_step: Pair,\nL227:     【221†220】) -> (A, A)\n"
